WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.149
Building injection-resistant data access in .

2
00:00:03.149 --> 00:00:05.178
NET is a repeatable workflow.

3
00:00:05.178 --> 00:00:08.397
Track data from entry point to query boundary.

4
00:00:08.397 --> 00:00:17.145
Understand when values come from users, APIs, files, queues, imports, stored records, configuration, or administrative tools.

5
00:00:17.145 --> 00:00:24.772
Stored data and internal workflow values still need careful handling when they influence database operations.

6
00:00:24.772 --> 00:00:29.321
Use parameterized commands and framework-supported safe patterns.

7
00:00:29.321 --> 00:00:30.720
ADO.

8
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:42.337
NET, EF Core, micro-ORMs, and stored procedures can all support safe designs when command structure remains under application control and values are passed as values.

9
00:00:42.337 --> 00:00:47.865
The exact technology matters less than preserving that separation consistently.

10
00:00:47.865 --> 00:00:51.014
Keep SQL structure under application control.

11
00:00:51.014 --> 00:01:08.508
Validate business input, allow-list query-shaping options, map caller choices to approved internal expressions, use paging and bounded ranges, and avoid giving clients direct control over fields, fragments, projections, or database command structure.

12
00:01:08.508 --> 00:01:11.797
Flexibility should be designed, not improvised.

13
00:01:11.797 --> 00:01:13.197
Layer the defense.

14
00:01:13.197 --> 00:01:15.786
Least privilege reduces blast radius.

15
00:01:15.786 --> 00:01:19.145
Secret management protects database credentials.

16
00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:21.314
Safe errors prevent disclosure.

17
00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:25.583
Structured logging and correlation IDs support investigation.

18
00:01:25.583 --> 00:01:32.511
Dependency review and legacy remediation keep the system from drifting back toward unsafe patterns.

19
00:01:32.511 --> 00:01:37.619
The practical takeaway is simple: make safe data access the easiest path.

20
00:01:37.619 --> 00:01:45.527
Document approved patterns, review high-risk query paths, maintain libraries, and test the behavior that matters.

21
00:01:45.527 --> 00:01:55.743
SQL injection prevention is not a one-time cleanup; it is an ordinary engineering habit that keeps data access predictable as applications evolve.
